%[text] # Multi-Beam Satellite Activation Optimization
%[text] Select which spot beams to activate in a fixed time-frequency resource block to maximize throughput without co-channel interference, solved first as a classical optimization problem and then through a QUBO/QAOA quantum optimization workflow.
%[text] This example is a teaching slice, not a quantum advantage benchmark. The one-slot hex-grid MWIS instance is deliberately small and structured enough for classical MILP to solve easily. The same QUBO pattern becomes more relevant as a building block in richer planning models with variables such as $x\_{b,g,f,t}$ for beam, gateway, frequency, and time-slot choices.
%[text] **Workflow:** Beam geometry -\> conflict graph -\> problem-based MILP baseline -\> QUBO reformulation -\> quantum (QAOA) solution -\> hardware next steps
%[text] **Requires:** MATLAB Support Package for Quantum Computing, Optimization Toolbox
%[text] ## Adapting This Template
%[text] You can reuse this example for other binary conflict problems by changing three pieces:
%[text] 1. **Instance model** - Replace the beam layout and demand pattern with your own assets, tasks, or regions.
%[text] 2. **Conflict model** - Replace the interference rule with your own incompatibility logic.
%[text] 3. **QUBO and result interpretation** - Replace the reward, penalty, and bit-string scoring terms so they reflect your objective, constraints, and domain metrics. \
%[text] If your problem is still "select a high-value subset subject to pairwise conflicts," the MILP, QUBO, tabu search, and QAOA solve pattern usually stay the same. The plots and hardware measurement summaries should be adapted to the new domain.
%%
%[text] ## 1. Problem Setup - Beam Geometry and Interference
%[text] A multi-beam satellite illuminates a service area with spot beams arranged in a hexagonal grid. Adjacent beams interfere if they are active on the same frequency channel or in the same shared time-frequency resource. This template uses the **beam activation** version of that problem: fix one candidate resource block, then choose which beams should be active in it.
%[text] With that modeling choice, the problem is a **maximum weight independent set** problem: pick the highest-demand subset of beams such that no two conflicting beams are both active in the same resource block.
%[text] ### Modeling note: activation versus frequency assignment
%[text] This template solves the single-resource activation model. The binary decision is $x\_i = 1$ if beam $i$ is active in this fixed time-frequency slot, so each conflict edge becomes $x\_i + x\_j \\le 1$.
%[text] If frequency is also a decision, use a larger graph-coloring style model with binary variables $x\_{i,f} = 1$ if beam $i$ uses frequency $f$. For optional activation, use $\\sum\_f x\_{i,f} \\le 1$ for each beam; if every beam must be assigned a frequency, use $\\sum\_f x\_{i,f} = 1$ instead.
%[text] Same-frequency interference is then expressed as $x\_{i,f} + x\_{j,f} \\le 1$ for every conflict edge $(i,j)$ and every frequency $f$. The QUBO extension uses one binary variable per beam-frequency pair and adds quadratic penalties for these same-frequency conflict constraints.
%%
%[text] ### Define beam positions
%[text] Place beam centers on a hex lattice. Each beam covers a circular cell; beams whose coverage overlaps create co-channel interference.
nRings = 1;
adjacencyThreshold = 1.15;
[beamX, beamY] = hexGrid(nRings);
nBeams = numel(beamX);
%%
%[text] ### Build the conflict graph and assign demand weights
%[text] Two beams conflict if their centers are closer than the interference threshold. This creates graph edges: if two beams share an edge, they cannot both be active in the same fixed frequency/time resource.
conflictPairs = findConflictPairs(beamX, beamY, adjacencyThreshold);
G = graph(conflictPairs(:,1), conflictPairs(:,2), [], nBeams);
%[text] Each beam has a demand weight representing user traffic in that cell. This example uses a tactical traffic pattern: a central hotspot, an east-facing gateway corridor, and slightly higher demand in the northern coverage area.
demand = beamDemandPattern(beamX, beamY, nRings);
demand = demand(:);
%[text] Show the domain first: each hex cell is a beam service area, color shows traffic demand, and red links show pairs that cannot both use this same resource block.
figure %[output:9ae1fad8]
plotSingleResourceConflictMap(beamX, beamY, demand, conflictPairs) %[output:9ae1fad8]
%%
%[text] ## 2. Classical Baseline - Problem-Based Integer Linear Program
%[text] The problem-based Optimization Toolbox API lets you write the model in terms of decision variables, objective, and constraints. Here, `activation(i) = 1` means beam `i` is active in the fixed resource block.
%[text] $\\max \\sum\_i w\_i x\_i \\quad \\text{s.t.} \\quad x\_i + x\_j \\le 1 \\; \\forall (i,j) \\in \\text{conflicts}, \\quad x\_i \\in \\{0,1\\}$
%%
activation = optimvar("activation", nBeams, Type="integer", LowerBound=0, UpperBound=1);
beamProblem = optimproblem(ObjectiveSense="maximize");
beamProblem.Objective = dot(demand, activation);
noInterference = optimconstr(size(conflictPairs, 1), 1);
for k = 1:size(conflictPairs, 1)
    i = conflictPairs(k, 1);
    j = conflictPairs(k, 2);
    noInterference(k) = activation(i) + activation(j) <= 1;
end
beamProblem.Constraints.NoCoChannelInterference = noInterference;
milpOptions = optimoptions("intlinprog", Display="off");
[classicalSolution, ~, exitflag_classical] = solve(beamProblem, Options=milpOptions);
x_classical = classicalSolution.activation(:);
throughput_classical = dot(demand, x_classical);
[feasible_classical, violations_classical] = isActivationFeasible(x_classical, conflictPairs);
out_str = [
    sprintf("Classical problem-based MILP: activate beams %s", mat2str(find(x_classical).'))
    sprintf("Total throughput: %.1f", throughput_classical)
    sprintf("Solver status: %s", string(exitflag_classical))
    sprintf("Feasible solution: %s (%d conflict violations)", string(feasible_classical), violations_classical)
];
disp(join(out_str, newline)); %[output:65bf3748]
%%
%[text] ## 3. QUBO Reformulation - The Quantum Bridge
%[text] To solve this on a quantum computer, reformulate the constrained binary problem as a **Quadratic Unconstrained Binary Optimization** problem. 
%[text] The key concept: explicit conflict constraints become penalties in the objective.
%[text] ### Why QUBO?
%[text] A quantum annealer or QAOA circuit minimizes an energy landscape. Encode "do not activate conflicting beams in this same resource block" as a cost penalty: if two conflicting beams are both set to 1, the objective gets worse by a penalty amount $\\lambda$.
%[text] ### The mapping
%[text] The QUBO objective is $\\min x^T Q x$, where:
%[text] - Diagonal: $Q\_{ii} = -w\_i$ rewards activating beam $i$
%[text] - Off-diagonal: symmetric entries $Q\_{ij}=Q\_{ji}=+\\lambda/2$ for each conflict add a total $+\\lambda x\_i x\_j$ penalty \
%[text] Minimizing $x^T Q x$ maximizes throughput while penalizing constraint violations.
%%
%[text] ### Choose the penalty coefficient
%[text] $\\lambda$ must be large enough that violating a conflict is not worthwhile. A safe starting point is $\\lambda \> \\max(w\_i)$.
lambda = max(demand) + 1;
fprintf("Penalty coefficient lambda = %.1f (> max demand %.1f)\n", lambda, max(demand)) %[output:0aa153a8]
%%
%[text] ### Build the QUBO problem
%[text] This is the weighted maximum independent set pattern, so the local `mis2qubo` utility can build the QUBO directly from the conflict graph. Store demand as node weights; otherwise the helper would solve the unit-weight independent set problem.
G.Nodes.Weight = demand;
qprob = mis2qubo(G, Penalty=lambda);
Q = full(qprob.QuadraticTerm + diag(qprob.LinearTerm));
imagesc(Q) %[output:7cb08f96]
colorbar %[output:7cb08f96]
axis equal tight %[output:7cb08f96]
title("QUBO Matrix Q (blue = reward, red = penalty)") %[output:7cb08f96]
xlabel("Beam index") %[output:7cb08f96]
ylabel("Beam index") %[output:7cb08f96]
%%
%[text] ## 4. Solve QUBO with Tabu Search
%[text] Before going quantum, verify the QUBO formulation with the built-in classical QUBO heuristic. `tabuSearch` is available in the [MATLAB Support Package for Quantum Computing](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/quantum-computing.html) add-on.
result_tabu = solve(qprob);
x_tabu = result_tabu.BestX(:);
throughput_tabu = dot(demand, x_tabu);
[feasible_tabu, violations_tabu] = isActivationFeasible(x_tabu, conflictPairs);
out_str = [
    sprintf("Tabu search: activate beams %s", mat2str(find(x_tabu).'))
    sprintf("Throughput: %.1f (matches MILP: %s)", throughput_tabu, string(abs(throughput_tabu - throughput_classical) < 1e-6))
    sprintf("Feasible solution: %s (%d conflict violations)", string(feasible_tabu), violations_tabu)
    ];
disp(join(out_str, newline)); %[output:8d510b02]
%%
%[text] ## 5. Quantum Solution - QAOA
%[text] Now solve the same QUBO using the **Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm**.
%[text] QAOA is a hybrid quantum-classical algorithm: a parameterized quantum circuit encodes the problem, and a classical optimizer tunes circuit parameters to minimize the QUBO cost. 
%[text] ### High-level solve
%[text] The simplest path is one function call with the same QUBO problem object used by tabu search.
rng default
qaoaOptions = optimset("Display", "off");
result_qaoa = solve(qprob, Algorithm=qaoa(OptimizationSolverOptions=qaoaOptions));
x_qaoa = result_qaoa.BestX(:);
throughput_qaoa = dot(demand, x_qaoa);
[feasible_qaoa, violations_qaoa] = isActivationFeasible(x_qaoa, conflictPairs);
out_str = [
    sprintf("QAOA: activate beams %s", mat2str(find(x_qaoa).'))
    sprintf("Throughput: %.1f", throughput_qaoa)
    sprintf("Feasible solution: %s (%d conflict violations)", string(feasible_qaoa), violations_qaoa)
    ];
disp(join(out_str, newline)); %[output:53060980]
%%
%[text] ## 6. Validation and Engineering Comparison
%[text] ### Compare all solutions
optimal_tabu = feasible_tabu && abs(throughput_tabu - throughput_classical) < 1e-6;
optimal_qaoa = feasible_qaoa && abs(throughput_qaoa - throughput_classical) < 1e-6;
comparison = table(["Classical MILP";"QUBO tabu search";"QUBO QAOA"], ...
    {mat2str(find(x_classical).'); mat2str(find(x_tabu).'); mat2str(find(x_qaoa).')}, ...
    [throughput_classical; throughput_tabu; throughput_qaoa], ...
    [string(feasible_classical); string(feasible_tabu); string(feasible_qaoa)], ...
    ["ref"; string(optimal_tabu); string(optimal_qaoa)], ...
    VariableNames=["Method","ActiveBeams","Throughput","Feasible","Optimal"]);
disp(comparison) %[output:830f992a]
%%
%[text] ### Visualize the selected beams
%[text] Compare the active footprints returned by the classical MILP and QAOA. Thin gray links show potential conflicts; red links would mark any conflict violations.
tiledlayout(1, 2, TileSpacing="compact", Padding="compact") %[output:43b17d2d]
nexttile %[output:43b17d2d]
plotBeamActivationMap(beamX, beamY, conflictPairs, x_classical, ... %[output:43b17d2d]
    sprintf("Classical MILP (throughput = %.1f)", throughput_classical)) %[output:43b17d2d]
nexttile %[output:43b17d2d]
plotBeamActivationMap(beamX, beamY, conflictPairs, x_qaoa, ... %[output:group:31149fa7] %[output:43b17d2d]
    sprintf("QAOA (throughput = %.1f)", throughput_qaoa)) %[output:group:31149fa7] %[output:43b17d2d]
%%
%[text] ## 7. Next Steps - Running on Quantum Hardware
%[text] The high-level `solve(qprob,Algorithm=qaoa)` call does more than return a bit string. The local QAOA solve uses simulation during the hybrid quantum-classical optimization and stores the optimized gate-level `quantumCircuit` in the result object. A quantum device runs circuits, not `qubo` objects, so this returned circuit is the object to submit for a first hardware run.
%%
%[text] ### Inspect the returned QAOA circuit
%[text] The returned circuit already contains the optimized QAOA angles. Its qubit and gate counts give a first hardware-resource check before choosing a backend.
beamQAOACircuit = result_qaoa.AlgorithmResult.AlgorithmInformation.Circuit;
figure %[output:470f8008]
plot(beamQAOACircuit) %[output:470f8008]
title("QAOA Circuit Returned by solve") %[output:470f8008]
qaoaHardwareSummary = table(beamQAOACircuit.NumQubits, numel(beamQAOACircuit.Gates), ...
    VariableNames=["NumQubits","NumGates"]);
disp(qaoaHardwareSummary) %[output:475c8258]
%%
%[text] ### Inspect the QAOA state probabilities
%[text] The QAOA solve already simulated the optimized circuit and stores the full state-probability distribution. Convert those bit states and probabilities into the same format expected from hardware measurements, then score them in the original beam-activation terms. The first table shows the most likely raw bit strings; the second filters to the best feasible beam-activation states.
qaoaStateBits = result_qaoa.AlgorithmResult.AllX.';
qaoaStates = join(string(qaoaStateBits), "", 2);
qaoaProbabilities = result_qaoa.AlgorithmResult.AlgorithmInformation.AllProbabilities(:);
qaoaStateSummary = scoreBeamActivationStateProbabilities( ...
    qaoaStates, qaoaProbabilities, qprob, demand, conflictPairs);
qaoaStateSummary = sortrows(qaoaStateSummary, "Probability", "descend");
topQAOAStates = qaoaStateSummary(1:min(8, height(qaoaStateSummary)), :);
disp(topQAOAStates) %[output:56c00df2]
qaoaValidStates = qaoaStateSummary(qaoaStateSummary.Feasible, :);
bestValidQAOAStates = sortrows(qaoaValidStates, "QUBOEnergy", "ascend");
bestValidQAOAStates = bestValidQAOAStates(1:min(5, height(bestValidQAOAStates)), :);
disp(bestValidQAOAStates) %[output:6c169e51]
%%
%[text] ### Commented hardware run
%[text] The blocks below are intentionally commented out so the template runs without cloud credentials. To use hardware, uncomment one device setup block, choose `hardwareDevice`, and submit the returned QAOA circuit directly. The key API difference is that `run(circuit,hardwareDevice,NumShots=...)` executes a circuit, while `solve(qprob,Algorithm=qaoa)` solves a local QUBO problem.
%{
% Amazon Web Services (AWS) setup
% https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/math/run-quantum-circuit-on-hardware-using-AWS.html
% Replace the ARN, region, and S3 path with your Braket configuration.
loadenv("awsConfig.env")
awsDevice = quantum.backend.QuantumDeviceAWS("PASTE_AWS_BRAKET_DEVICE_ARN", ...
    Region="PASTE_AWS_REGION", ...
    S3Path="s3://amazon-braket-YOUR_BUCKET/YOUR_PREFIX");

% IBM Quantum Compute Service setup
% https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/math/run-quantum-circuit-on-hardware-using-IBM.html
% Replace the device name, JSON credential file, and account name.
ibmAccountName = "PASTE_IBM_ACCOUNT_NAME";
ibmDevice = quantum.backend.QuantumDeviceIBM("PASTE_IBM_DEVICE_NAME", ...
    FileName="ibm-credentials.json", ...
    AccountName=ibmAccountName, ...
    UseSession=false);

% Choose one configured device for the hardware run.
hardwareDevice = awsDevice;

% Run the locally optimized QAOA circuit on hardware and translate the
% measured states back to beam activations, throughput, and feasibility.
numShots = 3000;
hardwareTask = run(beamQAOACircuit, hardwareDevice, NumShots=numShots);
wait(hardwareTask)
hardwareMeasurement = fetchOutput(hardwareTask);
hardwareStates = hardwareMeasurement.MeasuredStates(:);
hardwareProbabilities = hardwareMeasurement.Probabilities(:);
if isempty(hardwareProbabilities) || all(isnan(hardwareProbabilities))
    hardwareCounts = hardwareMeasurement.Counts(:);
    hardwareProbabilities = hardwareCounts/sum(hardwareCounts);
end
hardwareCandidates = scoreBeamActivationStateProbabilities( ...
    hardwareStates, hardwareProbabilities, qprob, demand, conflictPairs);
hardwareCandidates = sortrows(hardwareCandidates, "Probability", "descend");
topHardwareCandidates = hardwareCandidates(1:min(10, height(hardwareCandidates)), :)
%}
%%
%[text] ## Local Functions
%%
function conflictPairs = findConflictPairs(beamX, beamY, adjacencyThreshold)
%findConflictPairs Return all beam pairs whose centers are close enough to interfere.
    coords = [beamX(:), beamY(:)];
    dx = coords(:, 1) - coords(:, 1).';
    dy = coords(:, 2) - coords(:, 2).';
    D = sqrt(dx.^2 + dy.^2);
    [row, col] = find(triu(D < adjacencyThreshold & D > 0, 1));
    if isempty(row)
        conflictPairs = zeros(0, 2);
    else
        conflictPairs = [row col];
    end
end
function scoredStates = scoreBeamActivationStateProbabilities(states, probabilities, qprob, demand, conflictPairs)
%scoreBeamActivationStateProbabilities Convert state probabilities to beam metrics.
    states = string(states(:));
    demand = demand(:);
    probabilities = probabilities(:);
    probabilities = probabilities/sum(probabilities);
    numStates = numel(states);
    quboEnergy = zeros(numStates, 1);
    throughput = zeros(numStates, 1);
    feasible = false(numStates, 1);
    conflictViolations = zeros(numStates, 1);
    activeBeams = strings(numStates, 1);
    for row = 1:numStates
        x = stateToActivationVector(states(row));
        quboEnergy(row) = evaluateQUBOEnergy(qprob, x);
        throughput(row) = dot(demand, x);
        [feasible(row), conflictViolations(row)] = isActivationFeasible(x, conflictPairs);
        activeBeams(row) = string(mat2str(find(x).'));
    end
    scoredStates = table(states, probabilities, quboEnergy, throughput, feasible, ...
        conflictViolations, activeBeams, ...
        VariableNames=["State", "Probability", "QUBOEnergy", "Throughput", ...
        "Feasible", "ConflictViolations", "ActiveBeams"]);
end
function energy = evaluateQUBOEnergy(qprob, x)
%evaluateQUBOEnergy Evaluate the QUBO objective for one activation vector.
    x = double(x(:));
    energy = x.'*qprob.QuadraticTerm*x + qprob.LinearTerm(:).'*x + qprob.ConstantTerm;
end
function x = stateToActivationVector(state)
%stateToActivationVector Convert a measured bit string to a binary column vector.
    bits = char(string(state));
    x = double(bits(:) == '1');
end
function plotSingleResourceConflictMap(beamX, beamY, demand, conflictPairs)
%plotSingleResourceConflictMap Draw demand-weighted cells with same-resource conflict links.
    beamX = beamX(:);
    beamY = beamY(:);
    demand = demand(:);
    hold on
    for k = 1:numel(beamX)
        radius = 0.50;
        theta = pi/6 + (0:5)*pi/3;
        hx = beamX(k) + radius*cos(theta);
        hy = beamY(k) + radius*sin(theta);
        fill(hx, hy, demand(k), EdgeColor=[0.18 0.22 0.24], LineWidth=1.1);
    end
    plotConflictLines(beamX, beamY, conflictPairs, [0.72 0.12 0.10], 1.5);
    scatter(beamX, beamY, 30, "w", "filled", MarkerEdgeColor=[0.12 0.15 0.17], LineWidth=0.8)
    for k = 1:numel(beamX)
        text(beamX(k), beamY(k), string(k), HorizontalAlignment="center", ...
            VerticalAlignment="middle", FontWeight="bold", FontSize=9, ...
            Color=[0.08 0.10 0.11]);
    end
    hold off
    axis equal off
    colormap(turbo)
    cb = colorbar;
    ylabel(cb, "Traffic demand weight (not frequency)")
    title("Single-Resource Beam Demand and Conflict Map")
end
function plotBeamActivationMap(beamX, beamY, conflictPairs, x, titleText)
%plotBeamActivationMap Draw selected beam footprints and highlight conflict violations.
    beamX = beamX(:);
    beamY = beamY(:);
    x = logical(x(:));
    inactiveIdx = find(~x).';
    activeIdx = find(x).';
    hold on
    plotConflictLines(beamX, beamY, conflictPairs, [0.72 0.74 0.76], 0.7);
    for k = inactiveIdx
        drawBeamFootprint(beamX(k), beamY(k), 0.48, [0.78 0.81 0.84], [0.62 0.65 0.68], 0.30, 0.8);
    end
    for k = activeIdx
        drawBeamFootprint(beamX(k), beamY(k), 0.52, [0.06 0.48 0.72], [0.02 0.25 0.40], 0.62, 1.8);
    end
    if ~isempty(conflictPairs)
        violationPairs = conflictPairs(x(conflictPairs(:, 1)) & x(conflictPairs(:, 2)), :);
        plotConflictLines(beamX, beamY, violationPairs, [0.84 0.08 0.08], 2.6);
    end
    scatter(beamX(~x), beamY(~x), 24, [0.95 0.96 0.97], "filled", MarkerEdgeColor=[0.54 0.57 0.60], LineWidth=0.6)
    scatter(beamX(x), beamY(x), 42, [0.02 0.34 0.18], "filled", MarkerEdgeColor="w", LineWidth=0.8)
    for k = 1:numel(beamX)
        if x(k)
            labelColor = [1.00 1.00 1.00];
        else
            labelColor = [0.15 0.17 0.18];
        end
        text(beamX(k), beamY(k), string(k), HorizontalAlignment="center", ...
            VerticalAlignment="middle", FontWeight="bold", FontSize=9, ...
            Color=labelColor);
    end
    hold off
    axis equal off
    title(titleText)
end
function drawBeamFootprint(cx, cy, radius, faceColor, edgeColor, faceAlpha, lineWidth)
%drawBeamFootprint Draw one circular beam footprint as a translucent patch.
    theta = linspace(0, 2*pi, 80);
    patch(cx + radius*cos(theta), cy + radius*sin(theta), faceColor, ...
        FaceAlpha=faceAlpha, EdgeColor=edgeColor, LineWidth=lineWidth);
end
function plotConflictLines(beamX, beamY, conflictPairs, lineColor, lineWidth)
%plotConflictLines Draw pairwise conflict links between beam centers.
    for k = 1:size(conflictPairs, 1)
        pair = conflictPairs(k, :);
        plot(beamX(pair), beamY(pair), "-", Color=lineColor, LineWidth=lineWidth);
    end
end
function demand = beamDemandPattern(beamX, beamY, nRings)
%beamDemandPattern Deterministic tactical demand model used by the example.
    beamX = beamX(:);
    beamY = beamY(:);
    if nRings == 1
        centerSigma = 0.70;
        gatewaySigma = 0.55;
        gatewayX = 1.0;
        gatewayY = -0.35;
    else
        centerSigma = max(0.70, 0.25*nRings);
        gatewaySigma = max(0.55, 0.20*nRings);
        gatewayX = 0.60*nRings;
        gatewayY = -0.20*nRings;
    end
    centerHotspot = exp(-(beamX.^2 + beamY.^2) / (2*centerSigma^2));
    gatewayHotspot = exp(-((beamX - gatewayX).^2 + (beamY - gatewayY).^2) / (2*gatewaySigma^2));
    northernBias = 0.6 * (beamY > 0);
    demand = round(2.0 + 4.8*centerHotspot + 3.6*gatewayHotspot + northernBias, 1);
end
function [isFeasible, violationCount] = isActivationFeasible(x, conflictPairs)
%isActivationFeasible Check whether an activation vector violates any beam conflicts.
    x = logical(x(:));
    if isempty(conflictPairs)
        violationCount = 0;
        isFeasible = true;
        return
    end
    violationMask = x(conflictPairs(:, 1)) & x(conflictPairs(:, 2));
    violationCount = nnz(violationMask);
    isFeasible = violationCount == 0;
end
function [bx, by] = hexGrid(nRings)
%hexGrid Generate beam center positions on a hexagonal lattice with unit spacing.
    bx = 0;
    by = 0;
    directions = [1 0; 0.5 sqrt(3)/2; -0.5 sqrt(3)/2; ...
                  -1 0; -0.5 -sqrt(3)/2; 0.5 -sqrt(3)/2];
    for ring = 1:nRings
        cx = ring * directions(1, 1);
        cy = ring * directions(1, 2);
        for side = 1:6
            nextDir = mod(side + 1, 6) + 1;
            for step = 1:ring
                bx = [bx; cx]; %#ok<AGROW>
                by = [by; cy]; %#ok<AGROW>
                cx = cx + directions(nextDir, 1);
                cy = cy + directions(nextDir, 2);
            end
        end
    end
end

